# Build artefacts (host or container).
*.o
*.d
*.a
*.dylib

# Generated protobuf-c outputs (regenerated from proto/dsc/*.proto by
# the libds Makefile's gen-protos rule per RDSC.12.3).
runtime/src/libds/dsc/*.pb-c.c
runtime/src/libds/dsc/*.pb-c.h

# Generated RFCS fact-stream bodies (regenerated from proto/ds/*.proto by
# the libds Makefile's gen-protos rule, RFCS1a).
runtime/src/libds/rfcs/*.pb-c.c
runtime/src/libds/rfcs/*.pb-c.h

# The projected Makefile's record of the VERSION it last built (#639).
runtime/src/libds/version.stamp

# A rejected download vendor_source.py keeps beside a pinned file (#1916).
*.rejected

# Test binaries — they live next to their .c sources after build.
runtime/tests/libds/impl/test_*
!runtime/tests/libds/impl/test_*.c
!runtime/tests/libds/impl/test_*.h
runtime/tests/libds/spec/test_*
!runtime/tests/libds/spec/test_*.c

# Python build artefacts (compiler tree + pyds tree).
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.so
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
build/
dist/
builds/
.eggs/
# A released compiler + protos unpack here, versioned by what was fetched, so
# `dist/` does not cover it. Untracked AND unignored is a
# `git add -A` hazard in a tree shared with concurrent agents.
dist-*/

# meson-python editable-install artefacts (pyds).
bindings/python/build/
bindings/python/.mesonpy*

# jupyter-book renders INTO the book's own source dir, on the host clone. `build/`
# above does not match `_build`. Load-bearing for build-unit, not just hygiene: source globs
# skip git-ignored paths, so this is what keeps a book's render out of its own
# declared inputs (docs/ds_docs.build).
_build/

# The pass-1 render (XBOOK1). The cross-book link graph is cyclic, so every book
# is rendered once into `_inv/` purely to emit its objects.inv, then again for
# real against the others' inventories. Ignored for the same load-bearing reason
# as `_build/` above: it is a render's own output living inside its source dir.
_inv/

# The reference books' catalogue tables, generated into each book's `_generated/`
# by scripts/spec/catalogues.py (#4377). Never committed, so never stale; and
# ignored for the same load-bearing reason as `_build/`: the books' source globs
# prune it, and the renders read the files as the catalogues step's outputs.
_generated/

# The local artifact directory. Builds write their outputs here, the rendered
# docs site among them (out/flash-*.stamp, out/bench-*.jsonl).
#
# Untracked and unignored, a docs build leaves the whole rendered site — 1,543
# files — in the working tree. Plain `git status` shows ONE `?? out/` line, because git
# collapses a wholly-untracked directory, so it reads as clean while `git status -uall`
# shows all 1,543, and `git add -A` would sweep the rendered site into a commit.
#
# Trailing slash on purpose: bare `out` would also match a FILE named `out` anywhere.
out/

# todo / mem CLI runtime lockfiles.
.mem.db.lock
.todo.db.lock

# Claude Code's per-checkout state. Nothing in it belongs in the repository;
# 04c238e4c committed its scheduled-task lock by accident.
.claude/
bindings/python/src/pyds/_pyds*.so

# protoc-generated DSC bindings (regenerated per ICOM.11.2; W8).
# The directory's __init__.py is committed (sys.path shim); only the
# generated *_pb2.py files are gitignored — regenerated at build
# (scripts/gen_protos.sh for local dev), never committed.
compiler/ds_compiler/dsc/proto_gen/*_pb2.py
compiler/ds_compiler/dsc/proto_gen/*_pb2.pyi

# Per-checkout dev-tool configuration and databases. The `mem` and `todo`
# stores live outside the repository, and each checkout's .mem.ini and
# .todo.ini say where; they are local state like the databases.
.mem.ini
.todo.ini
.mem.db
.mem.db.bak
.todo.db
.todo.db.bak
.todo.db.predelete
# todo graph render — regenerable via `todo graph`.
todo-graph.dot
todo-graph.png


# build-unit check-stamps from libds.build's `make all` (lint / harness-run / all).
runtime/src/libds/build_*.stamp

# build-unit's gate stamps, written at the repo root. A local build leaves a
# score of them.
*.stamp

# Per-gate junit documents (P5): the `Report` Output each pytest lane declares.
# The harness parses them into CaseFailed facts; the record is the durable copy.
*.junit.xml

# Captured gate output redirected to the repo root. One of these
# (.gateC.out — a FAILING `spec` run from the spec-ratify branch) was committed
# and sat in history for a day. A gate log is a transcript of one run against
# one commit on one host: it is stale the moment it lands, and the durable copy
# is the .buildstate record the run already writes. Ignore the shape so a
# `> .gateX.out` while iterating can never become a commit.
.gate*.out

# v2's state directory — one .buildstate record per run plus the cache index.
# Both are regenerable: the index is disposable by construction, and a record
# accounts for a run that has already happened.
.build/

# Scratch working notes (commit-message drafts, conformance logs, audit
# notes, ad-hoc analysis dumps). Local-only; never part of history.
scratch/

# accidental Claude Code transcript dumps
spacetime.txt
time.txt

# systemate's generated state, under a target's .systemate/ (systemate's
# docs/records.md). Listed by name rather than excluding .systemate/ and
# re-including lib/: naming the generated set means a NEW generated directory
# shows up in `git status` and somebody notices, while exclude-then-re-include
# means a new SOURCE directory is silently untracked and only surfaces when
# another host pulls and the graphs are not there. One mistake is loud; the
# other is not.
**/.systemate/run/
**/.systemate/transcripts/
**/.systemate/channels/
**/.systemate/flags/
**/.systemate/traces/

# Compiled rule artefacts are build OUTPUTS, not sources (RPDSCSTALE3, owner
# ruling 2026-08-27). REPO-WIDE, with no exemption: a tracked `.dsc` is a
# licence for the artefact to diverge from the compiler that must load it, and
# "golden vectors pinned to a released compiler" was considered and rejected for
# exactly that reason. A second runtime proves it reads what the compiler emits
# by compiling with THAT compiler, not against a frozen copy of an older one.
#
# The ignore rule is the whole mechanism — a `.dsc` cannot be committed, so it
# cannot go stale. There is deliberately no freshness checker: a check over a
# population that cannot exist is a check that cannot fail.
#
# NOT a licence to keep binaries elsewhere. The fuzz corpus stays base64 for its
# own reason — those bytes are deliberately malformed INPUTS and regenerating
# them destroys their value — and that encoding keeps it out of this pattern.
*.dsc

# Spec-corpus DUMPS. `ALL9.tsv` was 2.8 MB of every clause's `text` field, one
# row per clause file, committed 2026-07-31 and never regenerated — so by the
# time it was found it still carried retired protos and a `format_minor` from a
# ladder two majors old, in a file a reader could easily mistake for the corpus.
#
# The corpus is `specs/**/*.json` and the queryable view of it is the DuckDB
# substrate `scripts/private/spec/spec_db.py` builds on demand ([STYL.5.10.1]). A checked-in
# flat dump is a second copy of the source that nothing regenerates and no gate
# reads, which is the [STYL.5.6.2] shape that has already cost this repo 53
# proto pages and a projected Makefile.
#
# Pattern rather than the one filename, so the next dump cannot land under a
# new name.
/*.tsv
runtime/mercury/src/ds.version.m
